  "enhanced_information": "The patient's presentation of painful oral lesions, vesicular rash, conjunctival injection, and ocular symptoms, along with a recent upper respiratory infection, strongly suggests a herpes simplex virus (HSV) infection. Direct immunofluorescence analysis of perilesional skin biopsy is the gold standard for confirming HSV, as it can identify viral antigens in the skin lesions. Other options, such as biopsy and histopathologic analysis, may provide information on blistering conditions but are less specific for HSV. Gram stain and culture of skin samples are not appropriate for viral infections. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis can be confirmed through direct immunofluorescence analysis.",
